8. Data Isolation & Security

8.1 Multi-Tenant Data Isolation

-   Every data access (sessions, files, chat history, vector search) must be scoped by the authenticated user's ID.
-   Vector database queries must always include a userid filter — there must be no code path where a user's search could return chunks from another user's documents.
-   Session and file CRUD operations must verify ownership before executing.

8.2 Input Sanitization

-   Filenames must be sanitized (non-alphanumeric characters replaced) before being used in file system paths, preventing path traversal attacks.
-   Session IDs must be trimmed of whitespace before processing.

8.3 Token Security

-   Access tokens must be signed with a secret key and must include an expiration claim.
-   The frontend must store tokens in local storage and include them in every API request.
-   On logout, the token must be removed from local storage and all client-side state must be cleared.

8.4 CORS Policy

-   The backend must allow cross-origin requests from the frontend origin (currently configured as wildcard  for development).

---

9. Background Task Processing

9.1 Task Queue

-   Long-running document processing tasks must be dispatched to a background task queue (backed by Redis) rather than processed inline during the API request.
-   The task queue must support automatic retries (up to 3 times) for transient failures (e.g., temporary network issues with external APIs).

9.2 Worker Lifecycle

-   The background worker must run as a separate process from the API server.
-   The worker must properly dispose of database connections on shutdown to avoid connection leaks.
-   The worker must clean up temporary files in a finally block, ensuring cleanup happens even if the task throws an exception.

9.3 Status Reporting

-   The background worker must update the database record (SessionFile) on both success and failure, so the API server and frontend can report accurate status without direct communication with the worker.

---

10. External Service Dependencies

10.1 Document Parsing Service

-   The system requires an external document parsing API capable of high-resolution text extraction from PDFs (including OCR for scanned documents and complex layout analysis).
-   This is outsourced rather than done locally because high-quality PDF parsing (especially for tables, multi-column layouts, and scanned documents) requires specialized infrastructure.

10.2 Embedding Model

-   The system requires an embedding model API to convert text chunks into dense vector representations for similarity search.
-   The system must support a fallback embedding model in case the primary model is unavailable or deprecated. This prevents a single model deprecation from breaking the entire system.

10.3 Vector Database

-   The system requires a hosted vector database for storing and querying document chunk embeddings.
-   The collection must be auto-created on first use if it doesn't exist.
-   The collection must have payload indexes on userid, filename, and filehash for efficient filtered queries.

10.4 Reranking Service

-   The system requires a reranking API to improve the relevance ordering of retrieved chunks.
-   This must be a non-critical dependency — if the reranker fails, the system must fall back to the initial retrieval order rather than failing the entire query.

10.5 LLM Service

-   The system requires a generative language model API for producing natural language answers.
-   The LLM must support streaming output (token-by-token) to enable real-time response delivery.
-   The temperature must be set low (e.g., 0.2) to prioritize factual, deterministic responses over creative ones — this is a document QA system, not a creative writing tool.

10.6 Relational Database

-   The system requires a PostgreSQL database for storing user accounts, session metadata, chat history, and file tracking records.
-   The database connection must support async I/O, SSL encryption, automatic reconnection on stale connections, and periodic connection recycling to handle managed database services that enforce idle timeouts.

---

11. System Initialization & Startup

11.1 Database Schema

-   On application startup, the system must automatically create any missing database tables from the defined models. This enables zero-downtime schema evolution for additive changes (new tables/columns).

11.2 ML Model Warm-Up

-   On application startup, the intent classification model must be trained and loaded into memory before the first request is served. This prevents the first user query from experiencing a cold-start delay.

11.3 Task Broker Initialization

-   On application startup, the task broker must be initialized for dispatching background tasks. On shutdown, it must be cleanly shut down.

---

12. Developer Tooling & Maintenance

12.1 Database Reset

-   A standalone script must exist to drop all tables and recreate them from the ORM models. This is for development use only — it is a destructive operation that erases all user data, sessions, and file records.

12.2 Vector Database Reset

-   A standalone script must exist to delete the entire vector collection from the vector database. This is for development use only — it removes all indexed document chunks.

12.3 Environment Configuration

-   All external service credentials, database URLs, and security keys must be configurable via environment variables (loaded from a .env file). No credentials must be hardcoded in source code.


